    "Aggregation Explained" : {
      "comment" : "A title for a view that explains in detail how data is aggregated in the charts.",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Aggregation Explained"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Aggregation Explanation Long" : {
      "comment" : "A detailed explanation text for the aggregation method picker in the settings view.",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Entries are aggregated using the selected method to reduce the number of visible data points, making the chart easier to analyze and improving display performance. In both the weekly and monthly views, all entries for a given day are aggregated into a single data point. In the yearly view, all entries for a given month are aggregated into a single data point.\n\nFor example, if you entered the values 1, 2, and 3 all on the same day and choose the Sum method, that day will have a value of 1 + 2 + 3 = 6. However, if you choose the Average method, it will be (1 + 2 + 3) / 3 = 2, which is the average for that day.\n\nSum makes more sense for data that accumulates, such as push-ups or pages read. For example, if you are tracking how many pages you read and you read 5 pages in the morning, 10 pages in the afternoon, and 15 pages in the evening, it is probably more interesting to know that you read a total of 30 pages that day (Sum) than that the average entry for that day was 10.\n\nAverage makes more sense for data that does not accumulate, such as your weight. If you weigh 75 kg in week 1, 80 kg in week 2, and 85 kg in week 3, you probably don't want to know that you weighed 240 kg (Sum) that month, but rather that you weighed 80 kg on average."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Aggregation Explanation Short" : {
      "comment" : "A short explanation text for the aggregation method picker in the settings view.",
      "extractionState" : "manual",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Entries are aggregated using the selected method.\n- Sum makes more sense for data that accumulates, like pushups or pages read.\n- Average makes more sense for data that doesn't accumulate, like your weight."
          }
        }
      }
    },
